[Music]  as we continue our series i  on earth we're introducing you to one  organization that isn't waiting for  congress  to take action on climate change the  solutions project co-founded by  by award-winning actor mark ruffalo aims  to transition to 100 percent  clean renewable energy for everyone by  2050. that's coming up the project has  supported and funded more than 100  grassroots organizations ninety-five  percent of which are run by people of  color  my name is rauker matsian my name is  nathaniel smith i am suzanne singer i am  the executive director of native  renewables  and my grandparents were among the 15  000  families that lack access to electricity  on the navajo nation and that's why i  founded native renewables we've been  able to install several  off-grid solar systems for families and  that's been really exciting because we  can see them turn the light on for the  very first time  the partnership with southern equity  works to advance a new southern agenda  for racial equity and shared prosperity  but we're educating  communities of color to be more active  and involved  in their own destiny by way of advocacy  as relates to climate and energy issues  this is amazing  push buffalo is a frontline community  based organization that is mobilizing  residents to create strong neighborhoods  and we build affordable housing  we build community-owned renewable  energy projects everything we do  at push buffalo is rooted in addressing  climate justice our next project is  going to be 49 units of affordable  housing  with 30 supportive housing and they will  be built  with no fossil fuel infrastructure in  buffalo new york  we're grateful to the solutions project  they have just been not only a funder  but but a great ally it's a real  authentic  relationship that honors the wisdom that  frontline communities like ours know  what they want where they live  and that we're already leading with real  solutions for real people  mark ruffalo and the solutions project  president and ceo gloria walton join us  now  uh good morning to you uh mark i want to  start with you as a co-founder of the  solutions project  uh you know climate change is obviously  a global issue but the focus of the  project is on  local actors how did it come about and  why that strategy  uh well thanks for having us on um it's  great to be here  talking about the good news uh of of  uh this moment we're living in i i  moved my family to upstate new york  right in the  moment that um they were gonna put 55  000  uh fracking wells uh in my community  and so um my experience of this is is  from  the front line my community  had to fight um with very little  resources it was a rural community  and we had to fight literally for our  lives and our children's lives  it was poison the air and the water and  would create an enormous amount of  pollution and  what i saw was how efficient  how effective that kind of organizing  was  and um how little resources  you needed to actually create change and  we not only did we ban fracking but we  also pushed the state to 100  renewable energy with the um the most  progressive  um legislation in the nation  on transitioning away from dirty fossil  fuels yeah  and so i saw the power of  community organizing to actually affect  change and  um and implement the the um  the change that needs to happen in order  for us to  thrive in this new um climate reality  crisis hey gloria we just heard mark say  that he's happy to talk about the good  news of this particular moment that  we're living through i think a lot of  people will be surprised to hear there  is good news  from where you're sitting there as  president of the solutions project  what do you like what do you see  oh wow so so much  um to mark's point this is a  moment where we have the president of  the united states saying  environmental justice and that has  everything to do  with the decades of organizing and power  building in front line communities  i particularly feel like we're in a  moment um  that i should speak to right a moment of  racial reckoning  and you know i think about how racism  pollution and poverty are interconnected  and in this time of racial reckoning i  feel like climate justice really  is an opportunity to transform our  systems  in a way that leaves no communities  behind so  and i say this gloria i just want to say  because you say  racial justice is an essential part of  tackling climate change  can you make that connection how does  that work yes  great question tony so uh  it's no coincidence that the same  communities that are criminalized and  dying at the hands of police officers  who swore to protect and serve us are  the very same communities that are first  and worst impacted by climate change and  that's not by  chance so decisions have been made to  create a lot of conditions that black  indigenous asian american and pacific  islander and other people of color  communities are living with  and these communities have a history of  being under resourced  and it's not coincidence that these  communities are often  surrounded in living adjacent to  freeways  oil drilling fracking sites and even  petrochemical plants  and so the air quality is often bad and  highly polluted  and what that means is that these  communities have higher rates of asthma  and other respiratory illnesses cancer  and even higher rates of  mortality right than white communities  and so when you look at something like  cobit for example  and they're saying how these same  communities are disproportionately  impacted it's not coincidence that these  are the same communities that are on the  front lines of environmental degradation  right and so if your lungs are already  compromised  if you get covered you're going to be  one of the sickest right and so for  generations  uh environmental justice communities or  as we like to call it frontline  communities those most impacted and  closest to the problems  are often the first to the solutions and  in fact are innovating  transformative climate solutions that  are tackling multiple problems at once  so they're creating living wage grain  jobs  training opportunities affordable  housing powered by  solar addressing public health by  fighting for policies to improve air  quality  advocating to keep oil in the ground and  putting buffer zones between where  families live and children play and so  this is a moment where our government  and corporate industry leaders need to  be looking to the expertise and  innovation happening in front line  communities  it's time to invest and scale  community-centered and community-led  solutions  gloria walton mark ruffalo we appreciate  you both for joining us thank you very  much mark can i just say i loved you and  i know this much is true congratulations  on your mate you were so good  i thought if he doesn't win something's  wrong has nothing to do with climate  change but you were awesome  nice to meet you thank you very much i  know we got to go we gotta go thank you  both  we're watching cbs this morning we'll be  right back

